{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"c86edd8c4fa33afe","name":"card_unit_99th_kholm","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/Soviet/NavalWarfare/units/card_unit_99th_kholm","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"99th KHOLM","text":"Deployment: Lose 1 kredit at the start of your next turn.","flavorText":"The 99th Rifle Regiment was an infantry unit in service in the 33rd Rifle Division of the Red Army during WWII. The division participated in the Soviet invasion of Poland in September 1939 and in the occupation of Lithuania from June 1940. Stationed there when Germany invaded in June 1941, it fought desperate battles during the Baltic strategic defensive operation, ultimately retreating through Kaunas and across the Viliya River. During August 1941, the division fought offensive battles in the area east of Kholm, liberating a number of villages. However, in early September 1941, the Germans launched a powerful offensive and the division was forced to retreat. After being reduced to only 500 men, the division was reformed and fought in the Toropets-Kholm Offensive in early 1942, leading to the encirclement of the German forces in the Kholm pocket. Throughout 1942 and until September 1943, the division fought southeast of the city of Kholm. The 99th Rifle Regiment earned the title \"Kholm\" for its bravery during those combats. The 33rd Division later distinguished itself in key operations, notably the Leningrad-Novgorod Offensive, the Tartu Offensive and the liberation of Riga in 1944. In 1945, it advanced through Pomerania and played a crucial role in the Battle of Berlin, storming the city from the north and reaching the Reichstag. Postwar, it served in Germany as part of the occupation forces before being disbanded in 1947.","faction":"Soviet","type":"infantry","rarity":"Common","cost":1,"operationCost":0,"attack":2,"defense":2},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"Name":"card_unit_99th_kholm","title":{"Namespace":"card_soviet","Key":"card_unit_99th_kholm_title","SourceString":"99th KHOLM","LocalizedString":"99th KHOLM"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::infantry","attack":2,"defense":2,"range":1,"faction":"EFactionEnum::Soviet","kredits":1,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_soviet","Key":"card_unit_99th_kholm_text","SourceString":"Deployment: Lose 1 kredit at the start of your next turn.","LocalizedString":"Deployment: Lose 1 kredit at the start of your next turn."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_soviet","Key":"card_unit_99th_kholm_history","SourceString":"The 99th Rifle Regiment was an infantry unit in service in the 33rd Rifle Division of the Red Army during WWII. The division participated in the Soviet invasion of Poland in September 1939 and in the occupation of Lithuania from June 1940. Stationed there when Germany invaded in June 1941, it fought desperate battles during the Baltic strategic defensive operation, ultimately retreating through Kaunas and across the Viliya River. During August 1941, the division fought offensive battles in the area east of Kholm, liberating a number of villages. However, in early September 1941, the Germans launched a powerful offensive and the division was forced to retreat. After being reduced to only 500 men, the division was reformed and fought in the Toropets-Kholm Offensive in early 1942, leading to the encirclement of the German forces in the Kholm pocket. Throughout 1942 and until September 1943, the division fought southeast of the city of Kholm. The 99th Rifle Regiment earned the title \"Kholm\" for its bravery during those combats. The 33rd Division later distinguished itself in key operations, notably the Leningrad-Novgorod Offensive, the Tartu Offensive and the liberation of Riga in 1944. In 1945, it advanced through Pomerania and played a crucial role in the Battle of Berlin, storming the city from the north and reaching the Reichstag. Postwar, it served in Germany as part of the occupation forces before being disbanded in 1947.","LocalizedString":"The 99th Rifle Regiment was an infantry unit in service in the 33rd Rifle Division of the Red Army during WWII. The division participated in the Soviet invasion of Poland in September 1939 and in the occupation of Lithuania from June 1940. Stationed there when Germany invaded in June 1941, it fought desperate battles during the Baltic strategic defensive operation, ultimately retreating through Kaunas and across the Viliya River. During August 1941, the division fought offensive battles in the area east of Kholm, liberating a number of villages. However, in early September 1941, the Germans launched a powerful offensive and the division was forced to retreat. After being reduced to only 500 men, the division was reformed and fought in the Toropets-Kholm Offensive in early 1942, leading to the encirclement of the German forces in the Kholm pocket. Throughout 1942 and until September 1943, the division fought southeast of the city of Kholm. The 99th Rifle Regiment earned the title \"Kholm\" for its bravery during those combats. The 33rd Division later distinguished itself in key operations, notably the Leningrad-Novgorod Offensive, the Tartu Offensive and the liberation of Riga in 1944. In 1945, it advanced through Pomerania and played a crucial role in the Battle of Berlin, storming the city from the north and reaching the Reichstag. Postwar, it served in Germany as part of the occupation forces before being disbanded in 1947."},"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Common","cardSet":"ECardSetEnum::NavalWarfare","usedTriggers":["ERegisteredCardFunction::OnStartofTurn"],"draftBaseWeight":1.2,"suppressionExceptionTriggers":["ERegisteredCardFunction::OnStartofTurn"],"hasDeployment":true,"hasBlitz":true,"Image":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/Images/Soviet/NavalWarfare/t_99th_kholm.t_99th_kholm","SubPathString":""},"artist":"Volmi Games","historyPhoto":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/HistoricalPhotos/99th_KHOLM.99th_KHOLM","SubPathString":""},"historyPhotoSize":{"X":512.0,"Y":336.0},"historyPhotoDescriptio